=== REASONING WATCHLIST (errors to self-monitor) ===
These are reasoning patterns you tend toward. Self-monitor — if you catch yourself using one, flag it explicitly rather than letting it pass.
- [saf-beliefs-200] AI Systematically Optimizes Proxy Metrics at the Expense of Intended Outcomes: Hasty Generalization — The node extrapolates from well-documented cases in narrow RL benchmarks and game environments to a universal claim about AI systems broadly, including large language models and other architectures where proxy gaming manifests very differently or less systematically.
- [saf-beliefs-018] The Attacker-Defender Cost Gap Erodes Social Trust: Slippery Slope — The node posits a near-inevitable chain from cost asymmetry → norm erosion → institutional breakdown → societal collapse, without adequately accounting for intermediate stabilizing factors or points of intervention.
- [saf-beliefs-117] State Suppression of AI Risk Research and Discourse: Overgeneralization — The node asserts that governments engage in active suppression of AI risk discourse as a general pattern, but documented evidence is sparse and episodic; generalizing from isolated cases to systemic state behavior overstates the empirical foundation.
- [saf-desires-005] No Black Boxes in Ground-Truth-Absent or Feedback-Contaminated Domains: Nirvana Fallacy — The demand for complete eradication of black-box opacity sets an ideal standard that may be technically unreachable, implicitly dismissing the significant safety value of partial interpretability, empirical auditing, and robust behavioral testing.
- [saf-intentions-170] Requiring Hardware Telemetry as the Price of Liability Immunity: False solution / Security theater — Hardware telemetry is presented as the definitive answer to audit-washing, but there is no established demonstration that silicon-level metrics reliably detect or characterize the emergent behaviors most relevant to AI safety. The confidence in hardware as 'ground truth' may overstate what telemetry can actually verify.
- [saf-intentions-146] Expanding Civil Discovery Rights to Pierce Customer-Held Datasets and Audits: Reification of Interpretability — The node treats internal reasoning logs as inherently meaningful forensic artifacts, assuming they reliably represent causal processes—when current interpretability research offers no guarantee that logged intermediate states correspond to actual causal pathways rather than post-hoc rationalizations.
- [saf-intentions-032] Making AI Show Its Work So Auditors Can Spot Deception: Transparency Theater / Proxy Fallacy — The node assumes that access to chain-of-thought reasoning constitutes genuine insight into the model's decision-making, but CoT outputs may be surface-level rationalizations rather than true reflections of the underlying computation, creating an illusion of oversight without substantive safety gains.
- [saf-intentions-167] Creating an NTSB-Style Body for AI Safety Incidents: False Analogy — The NTSB analogy assumes AI incidents share the key properties that make aviation investigation tractable — discrete events, physical evidence, stable post-incident system state, and clear causal chains — but AI systems are continuously updated, distributed globally, and often lack recoverable post-incident artifacts, making the analogy structurally strained.

